IMPORT prelude

-- Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1993 (Australian Capital Territory) -- the ACT's part of the national
-- cross-vesting scheme: which other courts may hear an ACT civil matter (s 4); when a proceeding must or may be moved
-- between the ACT Supreme Court and the federal and other State and Territory courts (ss 5, 6, 6A, 8, 10); where an appeal
-- must be brought (s 7); which law the court applies (s 11); what happens to the steps and costs already taken (ss 11(3),
-- 12); no appeal from a transfer decision (s 13); and enforcing the judgment in the ACT (s 14).
--
-- SEAT: the party to a civil proceeding -- usually the claimant who brings it, sometimes the defendant who answers it (an
-- intervener counts: dictionary, "party ... includes a person who intervenes in the proceeding"). "My claim is in the ACT
-- Supreme Court. Must it go to the Federal Court? Can I get it moved to New South Wales? Where do I appeal? Can I appeal the
-- transfer? Which law applies?" The Act's provisions are addressed to courts; they are encoded as what they mean for the
-- party. Every opinion the Act leaves to a court ("it appears to the Supreme Court", "more appropriate", "the interests of
-- justice", "special reasons", "considers it appropriate") is asked as an input, never decided.
--
-- Edition: Republication No 4 (R4), effective 21 March 2025 (last amendment by A2025-3), ACT Legislation Register,
-- retrieved 6 November 2026, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/A1993-60.txt. No instrument sets a number or
-- date this Act uses.
--
-- "the Supreme Court" is the Supreme Court of the ACT (Legislation Act 2001, dictionary pt 1). In this Act "State" means
-- the ACT, the NT or a State, so "another State" is the NT or one of the six States, and "Territory" (which excludes the
-- ACT and the NT) is an external Territory. "Family Court means the Family Court of Australia": that court has been the
-- Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 1) since 1 September 2021; the Act's name for it is kept (see
-- NOTES.md finding 1).
--
-- NOT ENCODED (machinery, or nothing a party can ask): ss 1, 2 (name, dictionary); s 6A(6), (7) (no new jurisdiction;
-- reading "interests of justice"); s 14(3) (references in other ACT laws); ss 15, 16, 17 (suspension and cessation by
-- Executive declaration). The preamble is quoted in nothing. The Commonwealth and other States' cross-vesting Acts are
-- different laws and are not encoded here (the Commonwealth's has its own baseline beside this subject).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written from the party's seat with the 2026-10-18 defaults. No pipeline, no coverage table, no
-- independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1993 (ACT) -- the party to a civil proceeding`

§§ `Questions this encoding does not cover`

-- The stable l4 has no REFUSE. A question that can reach a gap answers with an `Answer`: either `answered` with the value,
-- or `not covered` with the reason.
DECLARE `Why not covered` IS ONE OF
    `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
    `the person is not a party to the proceeding (a party includes an intervener); this Act gives a non-party no application`
    `the court is not exercising cross-vested jurisdiction, so section 11 does not say which law it applies`
    `this Act does not say where this appeal lies: that is for the Act that created the court`
    `this Act says nothing of that decision`

GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH A TYPE
DECLARE `Answer` a IS ONE OF
    `answered`    HAS `the answer` IS AN a
    `not covered` HAS `because`    IS A `Why not covered`

§§ `The courts`

-- Dictionary: "State means the Australian Capital Territory, the Northern Territory or a State of the Commonwealth";
-- "Territory does not include the Australian Capital Territory or the Northern Territory" (so a Territory is an external
-- Territory: Norfolk Island, Christmas Island, the Cocos (Keeling) Islands); "Family Court means the Family Court of
-- Australia"; "State Family Court, in relation to a State, means a court of that State to which the Family Law Act 1975
-- (Cwlth), section 41 applies under a proclamation made under that Act, section 41 (2)" (the Family Court of Western
-- Australia).
-- s 3: "A reference in this Act, other than a reference in section 4 (3), to the Supreme Court of a State includes, if there
-- is a State Family Court of that State, a reference to that State Family Court."
-- "the Supreme Court" (Legislation Act 2001, dictionary pt 1): the Supreme Court of the ACT.
-- "a court of the Australian Capital Territory, other than the Supreme Court" (ss 8, 10): for example the Magistrates Court;
-- "a tribunal established by or under an Act" (s 8): an ACT Act (Legislation Act 2001), for example ACAT.
DECLARE `Court` IS ONE OF
    `the ACT Supreme Court`
    `the Federal Court of Australia`
    `the Family Court (the Family Court of Australia, now the FCFCOA Division 1)`
    `the Supreme Court of another State (one of the six States, or the Northern Territory)`
    `the Supreme Court of an external Territory`
    `the Family Court of Western Australia (a State Family Court)`
    `another ACT court, or a tribunal established under an ACT Act`
    `another court or tribunal`

GIVEN c IS A `Court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is the Federal Court or the Family Court` c IF
        c EQUALS `the Federal Court of Australia`
    OR  c EQUALS `the Family Court (the Family Court of Australia, now the FCFCOA Division 1)`

-- "the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory", read with s 3 (the State Family Court counts, except in s 4(3)).
GIVEN c IS A `Court`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory, read with s 3` c IF
        c EQUALS `the Supreme Court of another State (one of the six States, or the Northern Territory)`
    OR  c EQUALS `the Supreme Court of an external Territory`
    OR  c EQUALS `the Family Court of Western Australia (a State Family Court)`

§§ `s 4 -- can this court hear my ACT matter`

-- Dictionary: "ACT matter means a matter-- (a) in which the Supreme Court has jurisdiction otherwise than by reason of a law
-- of the Commonwealth or of another State; or (b) removed to the Supreme Court under section 8."
GIVEN `the ACT Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other State law` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the matter was removed to the ACT Supreme Court under section 8` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is an ACT matter` `the ACT Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other State law` `the matter was removed to the ACT Supreme Court under section 8` IF
        `the ACT Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other State law`
    OR  `the matter was removed to the ACT Supreme Court under section 8`

-- s 4: "(1) The Federal Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in respect of ACT matters. (2) The
-- Family Court has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in respect of ACT matters. (3) The Supreme Court of
-- another State or of a Territory has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in respect of ACT matters. (4)
-- The State Family Court of another State has and may exercise original and appellate jurisdiction in respect of ACT
-- matters. (5) Subsection (1), (2), (3) or (4) does not-- (a) invest the Federal Court, the Family Court or a Supreme Court
-- with; or (b) confer on any such court; jurisdiction in respect of criminal matters."
-- Dictionary: "proceeding does not include a criminal proceeding."
-- s 9: "The Supreme Court may-- (a) exercise jurisdiction (whether original or appellate) conferred on that court by this Act
-- or a law of the Commonwealth or a State relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction; and (b) hear and determine a proceeding
-- transferred to that court under this Act or such a law."
DECLARE `Can this court hear my ACT matter` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Federal Court has original and appellate jurisdiction in ACT matters (s 4(1))`
    `yes: the Family Court has original and appellate jurisdiction in ACT matters (s 4(2))`
    `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has original and appellate jurisdiction in ACT matters (s 4(3))`
    `yes: the State Family Court of another State has original and appellate jurisdiction in ACT matters (s 4(4))`
    `this Act gives the ACT Supreme Court nothing here: an ACT matter is by definition within its own jurisdiction, and s 9 lets it exercise whatever jurisdiction the Commonwealth's or another State's cross-vesting law gives it`
    `no: section 4 gives no court jurisdiction in criminal matters (s 4(5))`
    `no: the matter is not an ACT matter, so section 4 of this Act gives the court nothing (the Commonwealth's or another State's cross-vesting law may)`

@export I have a civil claim, or I am defending one, that belongs in the ACT Supreme Court. Can the court where it is filed (the Federal Court, the Family Court, another State's or Territory's Supreme Court) hear it?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court where your proceeding is filed or pending (the Northern Territory's Supreme Court is "another State" in this Act; an external Territory is Norfolk Island, Christmas Island or the Cocos (Keeling) Islands)
      `it is a criminal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter is a criminal prosecution or other criminal matter (this Act covers civil matters only)
      `the ACT Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other State law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the ACT Supreme Court could hear the matter under ACT law alone, without relying on a Commonwealth law or another State's law (for example a negligence claim over an accident in the ACT)
      `the matter was removed to the ACT Supreme Court under section 8` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter was removed into the ACT Supreme Court from a lower ACT court or an ACT tribunal by an order under section 8
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can this court hear my ACT matter`
DECIDE `Can this court hear my ACT matter question` IS
    IF `the court` EQUALS `another court or tribunal` OR `the court` EQUALS `another ACT court, or a tribunal established under an ACT Act`
    THEN `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
    ELSE IF `the court` EQUALS `the ACT Supreme Court`
         THEN `answered` `this Act gives the ACT Supreme Court nothing here: an ACT matter is by definition within its own jurisdiction, and s 9 lets it exercise whatever jurisdiction the Commonwealth's or another State's cross-vesting law gives it`
         ELSE IF `it is a criminal matter`
              THEN `answered` `no: section 4 gives no court jurisdiction in criminal matters (s 4(5))`
              ELSE IF (NOT (`is an ACT matter` `the ACT Supreme Court has jurisdiction apart from Commonwealth or other State law` `the matter was removed to the ACT Supreme Court under section 8`))
                   THEN `answered` `no: the matter is not an ACT matter, so section 4 of this Act gives the court nothing (the Commonwealth's or another State's cross-vesting law may)`
                   ELSE `answered` (`section 4 subsection` `the court`)

GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court`
GIVETH A `Can this court hear my ACT matter`
`section 4 subsection` `the court` MEANS
    IF `the court` EQUALS `the Federal Court of Australia`
    THEN `yes: the Federal Court has original and appellate jurisdiction in ACT matters (s 4(1))`
    ELSE IF `the court` EQUALS `the Family Court (the Family Court of Australia, now the FCFCOA Division 1)`
         THEN `yes: the Family Court has original and appellate jurisdiction in ACT matters (s 4(2))`
         ELSE IF `the court` EQUALS `the Family Court of Western Australia (a State Family Court)`
              THEN `yes: the State Family Court of another State has original and appellate jurisdiction in ACT matters (s 4(4))`
              ELSE `yes: the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory has original and appellate jurisdiction in ACT matters (s 4(3))`

§§ `s 5 -- must my proceeding be transferred to another court`

-- s 5(1) (the ACT Supreme Court to the Federal Court or the Family Court), (2) (the ACT Supreme Court to the Supreme Court
-- of another State or of a Territory), (3) (the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory to the ACT Supreme Court),
-- (4) (the Federal Court or the Family Court to the ACT Supreme Court), (5) (between the Federal Court and the Family Court).
-- Each says that if the proceeding is pending in the first court "and it appears to the first court that-- (i) the relevant
-- proceeding arises out of, or is related to, another proceeding pending in [the other court] and it is more appropriate that
-- the relevant proceeding be determined by [it]; or (ii) having regard to-- (A) whether ... the relevant proceeding or a
-- substantial part of [it] would have been incapable of being instituted in [the first court] and capable of being
-- instituted in [the other court]; and (B) the extent to which ... the matters for determination ... are matters arising
-- under or involving questions as to the application, interpretation or validity of a law of [the other court's polity] and
-- not within the jurisdiction of [the first court] apart from this Act ...; and (C) the interests of justice; it is more
-- appropriate that the relevant proceeding be determined by [the other court]; or (iii) it is otherwise in the interests of
-- justice that the relevant proceeding be determined by [the other court]; the [first court] must transfer the relevant
-- proceeding to [the other court]." s 5(5) has only (i) and "(ii) it is otherwise in the interests of justice".
-- "Supreme Court of another State" includes the Family Court of Western Australia in s 5(2), (3) (s 3).
-- s 6A(3): "Section 5 (4) does not apply to a federal matter proceeding mentioned in subsection (2)" [one pending in the
-- Federal Court or Family Court]. s 6A(4): if the federal matter proceeding is pending in the Supreme Court and the ACT matter
-- proceeding is pending in any ACT court, "sections 5 (1) and 6 do not require the Supreme Court to transfer the federal
-- matter proceeding to the Federal Court or Family Court." (5): "However, the Supreme Court may transfer the federal matter
-- proceeding ... if it considers it appropriate". (2): the Federal Court or Family Court "may transfer the proceeding to the
-- Supreme Court" if it considers it appropriate.
DECLARE `Must my proceeding be transferred` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the ACT Supreme Court must transfer it to the Federal Court or the Family Court, as the case may be (s 5(1))`
    `yes: the ACT Supreme Court must transfer it to that other Supreme Court (s 5(2))`
    `yes: that other Supreme Court must transfer it to the ACT Supreme Court (s 5(3))`
    `yes: the Federal Court or the Family Court must transfer it to the ACT Supreme Court (s 5(4))`
    `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the Federal Court and the Family Court (s 5(5))`
    `not required: section 6A applies, so section 5(1) does not require the ACT Supreme Court to transfer my federal matter proceeding; it may still transfer it if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(4), (5))`
    `not required: section 6A applies, so section 5(4) does not apply; the Federal Court or the Family Court may transfer my proceeding to the ACT Supreme Court if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(2), (3))`
    `no: none of the grounds in section 5 appears to the court, so section 5 does not require a transfer`

-- Which subsection joins the two courts, if any (1 to 5; 0 for none).
GIVEN `from` IS A `Court`, `to` IS A `Court`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`section 5 subsection` `from` `to` MEANS
    IF `from` EQUALS `the ACT Supreme Court` AND `is the Federal Court or the Family Court` `to`
    THEN 1
    ELSE IF `from` EQUALS `the ACT Supreme Court` AND `is the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory, read with s 3` `to`
         THEN 2
         ELSE IF `is the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory, read with s 3` `from` AND `to` EQUALS `the ACT Supreme Court`
              THEN 3
              ELSE IF `is the Federal Court or the Family Court` `from` AND `to` EQUALS `the ACT Supreme Court`
                   THEN 4
                   ELSE IF `is the Federal Court or the Family Court` `from` AND `is the Federal Court or the Family Court` `to` AND (NOT (`from` EQUALS `to`))
                        THEN 5
                        ELSE 0

@export My civil proceeding is in one court and could be heard in another (the ACT Supreme Court, the Federal Court, the Family Court, or another State's Supreme Court). Must the court transfer it?
GIVEN `the court my proceeding is in` IS A `Court` @desc The court where your proceeding is pending
      `the other court` IS A `Court` @desc The court it might be transferred to
      `it appears to the court that a related proceeding is in the other court and it is more appropriate there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court where your proceeding is considers that it arises out of or is related to a proceeding pending in the other court, and that the other court is the more appropriate one
      `it appears to the court more appropriate there, weighing jurisdiction, the law involved and the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court considers the other court more appropriate, having regard to whether your proceeding (or a substantial part) could only have been brought there, how far it turns on the other place's law, and the interests of justice (not a ground between the Federal Court and the Family Court)
      `it appears to the court otherwise in the interests of justice to transfer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court considers it is otherwise in the interests of justice that the other court decide your proceeding
      `section 6A applies to my proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether section 6A applies (judicial review of a Commonwealth body or officer acting under an ACT law, with a related ACT proceeding pending: see the section 6A question)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Must my proceeding be transferred`
DECIDE `Must my proceeding be transferred question` IS
    IF `section 5 subsection` `the court my proceeding is in` `the other court` EQUALS 0
    THEN `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
    ELSE IF `section 6A applies to my proceeding` AND `section 5 subsection` `the court my proceeding is in` `the other court` EQUALS 1
         THEN `answered` `not required: section 6A applies, so section 5(1) does not require the ACT Supreme Court to transfer my federal matter proceeding; it may still transfer it if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(4), (5))`
         ELSE IF `section 6A applies to my proceeding` AND `section 5 subsection` `the court my proceeding is in` `the other court` EQUALS 4
              THEN `answered` `not required: section 6A applies, so section 5(4) does not apply; the Federal Court or the Family Court may transfer my proceeding to the ACT Supreme Court if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(2), (3))`
              ELSE IF (NOT (`a section 5 ground appears` (`section 5 subsection` `the court my proceeding is in` `the other court`) `it appears to the court that a related proceeding is in the other court and it is more appropriate there` `it appears to the court more appropriate there, weighing jurisdiction, the law involved and the interests of justice` `it appears to the court otherwise in the interests of justice to transfer`))
                   THEN `answered` `no: none of the grounds in section 5 appears to the court, so section 5 does not require a transfer`
                   ELSE `answered` (`section 5 duty` (`section 5 subsection` `the court my proceeding is in` `the other court`))

-- s 5(5) has no "having regard to" ground: there (ii) is "otherwise in the interests of justice".
GIVEN `subsection` IS A NUMBER
      `related` IS A BOOLEAN
      `weighing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 5 ground appears` `subsection` `related` `weighing` `justice` IF
        `related`
    OR  `justice`
    OR  (`weighing` AND (NOT (`subsection` EQUALS 5)))

GIVEN `subsection` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Must my proceeding be transferred`
`section 5 duty` `subsection` MEANS
    IF `subsection` EQUALS 1
    THEN `yes: the ACT Supreme Court must transfer it to the Federal Court or the Family Court, as the case may be (s 5(1))`
    ELSE IF `subsection` EQUALS 2
         THEN `yes: the ACT Supreme Court must transfer it to that other Supreme Court (s 5(2))`
         ELSE IF `subsection` EQUALS 3
              THEN `yes: that other Supreme Court must transfer it to the ACT Supreme Court (s 5(3))`
              ELSE IF `subsection` EQUALS 4
                   THEN `yes: the Federal Court or the Family Court must transfer it to the ACT Supreme Court (s 5(4))`
                   ELSE `yes: the court must transfer it to the other of the Federal Court and the Family Court (s 5(5))`

-- s 5(6): "If-- (a) a court (in this subsection called the first court) transfers a proceeding to another court under a law
-- or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction; and (b) it appears to the first court that-- (i) there is another
-- proceeding pending in the first court that arises out of, or is related to, the firstmentioned proceeding; and (ii) it is
-- in the interests of justice that the other proceeding be determined by the other court; the first court must transfer the
-- other proceeding to the other court."
DECLARE `Must my related proceeding follow` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court must transfer my related proceeding to the court it sent the first proceeding to (s 5(6))`
    `no: section 5(6) requires the related proceeding to follow only if the court considers it related and that the interests of justice call for it`
    `no: section 5(6) applies only once the court has transferred another proceeding under a cross-vesting law`

@export A court has transferred one proceeding to another court under the cross-vesting laws. Must my related proceeding in the same court go with it?
GIVEN `the court has transferred the first proceeding under a cross-vesting law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court where your proceeding is has transferred another proceeding to another court under this Act or another cross-vesting Act
      `it appears to the court that my proceeding arises out of or is related to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that court considers that your proceeding, still pending in it, arises out of or is related to the transferred one
      `it appears to the court that the interests of justice call for my proceeding to follow` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that court considers it in the interests of justice that the other court decide your proceeding too
GIVETH A `Must my related proceeding follow`
DECIDE `Must my related proceeding follow question` IS
    IF (NOT `the court has transferred the first proceeding under a cross-vesting law`)
    THEN `no: section 5(6) applies only once the court has transferred another proceeding under a cross-vesting law`
    ELSE IF `it appears to the court that my proceeding arises out of or is related to it` AND `it appears to the court that the interests of justice call for my proceeding to follow`
         THEN `yes: the court must transfer my related proceeding to the court it sent the first proceeding to (s 5(6))`
         ELSE `no: section 5(6) requires the related proceeding to follow only if the court considers it related and that the interests of justice call for it`

-- s 5(7): "A court may transfer a proceeding under this section on the application of a party to the proceeding, of its own
-- initiative or on the application of the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth or of a State or Territory."
-- Dictionary: "party, in relation to a proceeding, includes a person who intervenes in the proceeding."
DECLARE `Can I apply for a transfer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: as a party I may apply for a transfer under section 5; the court may also act of its own initiative, and an Attorney-General may apply (s 5(7))`

@export Can I ask the court to transfer my proceeding to another court under this Act?
GIVEN `I am a party to the proceeding or have intervened in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to the proceeding (a person who has intervened in it counts as a party)
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I apply for a transfer`
DECIDE `Can I apply for a transfer question` IS
    IF `I am a party to the proceeding or have intervened in it`
    THEN `answered` `yes: as a party I may apply for a transfer under section 5; the court may also act of its own initiative, and an Attorney-General may apply (s 5(7))`
    ELSE `not covered` `the person is not a party to the proceeding (a party includes an intervener); this Act gives a non-party no application`

-- s 5(8): "A person who is entitled to practise as a legal practitioner in a court has, if a proceeding (the transferred
-- proceeding) in that court is transferred to another court under a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction,
-- the same entitlement to practise in relation to-- (a) the transferred proceeding; and (b) any other proceeding out of which
-- the transferred proceeding arises or to which the transferred proceeding is related, being another proceeding that is to
-- be determined together with the transferred proceeding; in the other court that the person would have if the other court
-- were a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction."
DECLARE `Can my lawyer keep acting` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, for my transferred proceeding and any related proceeding to be decided with it, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8))`
    `no: section 5(8) gives that right only to a lawyer entitled to practise in the court the proceeding came from`
    `no: section 5(8) covers only the transferred proceeding and a related one to be decided together with it`
    `no: section 5(8) applies only to a proceeding transferred under a cross-vesting law`

@export My proceeding has been transferred to a court in another State or to a federal court. Can my lawyer keep acting for me there?
GIVEN `my proceeding was transferred under a cross-vesting law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding was transferred to the other court under this Act or another cross-vesting Act
      `my lawyer was entitled to practise in the court it came from` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your lawyer was entitled to practise as a legal practitioner in the court the proceeding was transferred from
      `it is the transferred proceeding or a related one to be decided with it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceeding your lawyer wants to act in is the transferred proceeding itself, or a related proceeding that is to be decided together with it
GIVETH A `Can my lawyer keep acting`
DECIDE `Can my lawyer keep acting question` IS
    IF (NOT `my proceeding was transferred under a cross-vesting law`)
    THEN `no: section 5(8) applies only to a proceeding transferred under a cross-vesting law`
    ELSE IF (NOT `my lawyer was entitled to practise in the court it came from`)
         THEN `no: section 5(8) gives that right only to a lawyer entitled to practise in the court the proceeding came from`
         ELSE IF (NOT `it is the transferred proceeding or a related one to be decided with it`)
              THEN `no: section 5(8) covers only the transferred proceeding and a related one to be decided together with it`
              ELSE `yes: my lawyer has the same right to practise in the new court, for my transferred proceeding and any related proceeding to be decided with it, as in a federal court exercising federal jurisdiction (s 5(8))`

§§ `s 6 -- special federal matters in the ACT Supreme Court`

-- Dictionary: "special federal matter--see the Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Cwlth), section 3 (1)." A
-- borrowed term (a signpost to a Commonwealth Act), so it is asked as a fact: which paragraph of the Commonwealth definition
-- the matter falls under, if any. The paragraphs (Commonwealth Act, as deposited at
-- subjects/au/jurisdiction-of-courts-cross-vesting-act-1987): (a) Part IV of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (other
-- than ss 45D-45EA); (aa) the Competition Code of the ACT or the NT; (ab) s 60G of the Family Law Act 1975 in a court other
-- than the Family Court of Western Australia or the NT Supreme Court; (b) questions of law on appeal from, or referred or
-- stated by, a Commonwealth tribunal, body or office-holder (with an exception); (c) the ADJR Act 1977; (e) the Federal
-- Court's original jurisdiction under s 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903; each "being a matter in respect of which the Supreme
-- Court of a State or Territory would not, apart from this Act, have jurisdiction". There is no paragraph (d).
DECLARE `Kind of special federal matter` IS ONE OF
    `(a) Part IV of the Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (restrictive trade practices)`
    `(aa) the Competition Code of the ACT or the Northern Territory`
    `(ab) section 60G of the Family Law Act 1975 (leave for a prescribed adoption)`
    `(b) questions of law on appeal from, or referred or stated by, a Commonwealth tribunal, body or office-holder`
    `(c) the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977`
    `(e) the Federal Court's original jurisdiction under section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903`
    `not a special federal matter`

-- s 6(1): "If-- (a) a matter for determination in a proceeding that is pending in the Supreme Court is a special federal
-- matter; and (b) the court does not make an order under subsection (3) in respect of the matter; the court must transfer the
-- proceeding in accordance with this section to the Federal Court or a court mentioned in subsection (2) (b)."
-- (2): "(a) if the matter ... is a matter mentioned in [the Commonwealth definition], paragraph (a), (b), (c) or (e)--to the
-- Federal Court; or (b) if ... paragraph (ab)--to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the
-- Supreme Court of the Northern Territory, in the opinion of the court, is appropriate in the circumstances." Paragraph (aa)
-- is in neither (NOTES.md finding 3).
-- (3): "The Supreme Court may order that the proceeding be determined by that court if it is satisfied that there are special
-- reasons for doing so in the particular circumstances of the proceeding other than reasons relevant to the convenience of
-- the parties." (4): before that order, notice to both Attorneys-General and a reasonable time (below).
-- s 6A(4), (5) (above): where s 6A applies, s 6 does not require the transfer; the Supreme Court may still transfer.
-- Unlike the Commonwealth's s 6(1A), the ACT s 6 transfers the whole proceeding (NOTES.md finding 4).
DECLARE `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the ACT Supreme Court must transfer my proceeding to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (2)(a))`
    `yes: the ACT Supreme Court must transfer my proceeding to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it thinks appropriate (s 6(1), (2)(b))`
    `yes, but the text names no receiving court: the matter is a special federal matter under paragraph (aa), which s 6(2) does not mention (s 6(1))`
    `no: the ACT Supreme Court has ordered, for special reasons, that it determine the proceeding itself (s 6(3)); it can do so only after notice to both Attorneys-General and a reasonable time (s 6(4))`
    `not required: section 6A applies, so section 6 does not require the ACT Supreme Court to transfer my federal matter proceeding; it may still do so if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(4), (5))`
    `no: the matter is not a special federal matter`
    `no: section 6 applies only to a proceeding pending in the ACT Supreme Court`

@export My civil proceeding is in the ACT Supreme Court and raises a special federal matter (competition law, judicial review of a Commonwealth decision, a question of law from a Commonwealth tribunal). Must it be transferred to the Federal Court?
GIVEN `the court my proceeding is in` IS A `Court` @desc The court where your proceeding is pending
      `the kind of matter` IS A `Kind of special federal matter` @desc Which paragraph of the Commonwealth Act's definition of "special federal matter" (Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987, s 3(1)) a matter in your proceeding falls under, if any
      `the Supreme Court has ordered that it determine the proceeding itself` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the ACT Supreme Court has made an order under section 6(3), for special reasons, that it will decide the proceeding itself
      `section 6A applies to my proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether section 6A applies (judicial review of a Commonwealth body or officer acting under an ACT law, with a related ACT proceeding pending: see the section 6A question)
GIVETH A `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court`
DECIDE `Must my special federal matter go to a federal court question` IS
    IF (NOT (`the court my proceeding is in` EQUALS `the ACT Supreme Court`))
    THEN `no: section 6 applies only to a proceeding pending in the ACT Supreme Court`
    ELSE IF `the kind of matter` EQUALS `not a special federal matter`
         THEN `no: the matter is not a special federal matter`
         ELSE IF `section 6A applies to my proceeding` AND (`the kind of matter` EQUALS `(c) the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977` OR `the kind of matter` EQUALS `(e) the Federal Court's original jurisdiction under section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903`)
              THEN `not required: section 6A applies, so section 6 does not require the ACT Supreme Court to transfer my federal matter proceeding; it may still do so if it considers that appropriate (s 6A(4), (5))`
              ELSE IF `the Supreme Court has ordered that it determine the proceeding itself`
                   THEN `no: the ACT Supreme Court has ordered, for special reasons, that it determine the proceeding itself (s 6(3)); it can do so only after notice to both Attorneys-General and a reasonable time (s 6(4))`
                   ELSE IF `the kind of matter` EQUALS `(ab) section 60G of the Family Law Act 1975 (leave for a prescribed adoption)`
                        THEN `yes: the ACT Supreme Court must transfer my proceeding to whichever of the Family Court, the Family Court of Western Australia or the Supreme Court of the Northern Territory it thinks appropriate (s 6(1), (2)(b))`
                        ELSE IF `the kind of matter` EQUALS `(aa) the Competition Code of the ACT or the Northern Territory`
                             THEN `yes, but the text names no receiving court: the matter is a special federal matter under paragraph (aa), which s 6(2) does not mention (s 6(1))`
                             ELSE `yes: the ACT Supreme Court must transfer my proceeding to the Federal Court (s 6(1), (2)(a))`

§§ `s 6(4), (5) -- the notice to the Attorneys-General (only if the ACT Supreme Court is asked to keep a special federal matter)`

-- s 6(4): "Before making an order under subsection (3), the Supreme Court must be satisfied that-- (a) a written notice
-- specifying the nature of the special federal matter has been given to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the
-- Attorney-General of the Australian Capital Territory; and (b) a reasonable time has elapsed since the giving of the notice
-- for the Attorneys-General to consider whether submissions to the court should be made in relation to the proceeding."
-- (5): "For subsection (4), the Supreme Court-- (a) may adjourn the proceeding for such time as the court thinks necessary
-- and may make such order as to costs in relation to an adjournment as it thinks fit; and (b) may direct a party to the
-- proceeding to give a notice in accordance with that subsection."
-- The Act names no one who must give the notice and sets no time for it. A party is bound only when the court directs it,
-- and the direction's time limit is the court's, so it is an input (in days).
DECLARE `Notice party` IS ONE OF
    `I, the party the court directed`
    `the ACT Supreme Court`

DECLARE `Notice act` IS ONE OF
    `give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the ACT`
    `order that the proceeding be determined by the ACT Supreme Court`

GIVEN `the time the court allowed, in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Notice party` `Notice act`
DECIDE `s 6(5)(b) -- I must give the notice the court directed` `the time the court allowed, in days` IS
    PARTY `I, the party the court directed`
    MUST  `give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the ACT`
    WITHIN `the time the court allowed, in days`
    HENCE
        PARTY `the ACT Supreme Court`
        MAY   `order that the proceeding be determined by the ACT Supreme Court`
    LEST  BREACH BY `I, the party the court directed` BECAUSE "s 6(5)(b): the court directed me to give the s 6(4) notice to both Attorneys-General and I did not give it in the time the court allowed"

DECLARE `Must I notify the Attorneys-General` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court has directed me to give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the ACT, in the time it allowed (s 6(4)(a), (5)(b))`
    `not unless the court directs me: the Act names no one who must give the notice, but the court cannot keep the matter until it is satisfied the notice was given and a reasonable time has passed (s 6(4))`
    `no: the notice is needed only where a special federal matter is pending in the ACT Supreme Court and it is asked to keep it (s 6(3), (4))`

@export My proceeding in the ACT Supreme Court involves a special federal matter and someone wants the Supreme Court to keep it. Must I give notice to the Attorneys-General?
GIVEN `my proceeding in the ACT Supreme Court involves a special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding, pending in the ACT Supreme Court, involves a special federal matter (see the section 6 question)
      `the court has directed me to give the notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has directed you, under section 6(5)(b), to give the notice to the Attorneys-General
GIVETH A `Must I notify the Attorneys-General`
DECIDE `Must I notify the Attorneys-General question` IS
    IF (NOT `my proceeding in the ACT Supreme Court involves a special federal matter`)
    THEN `no: the notice is needed only where a special federal matter is pending in the ACT Supreme Court and it is asked to keep it (s 6(3), (4))`
    ELSE IF `the court has directed me to give the notice`
         THEN `yes: the court has directed me to give written notice of the nature of the special federal matter to the Attorney-General of the Commonwealth and the Attorney-General of the ACT, in the time it allowed (s 6(4)(a), (5)(b))`
         ELSE `not unless the court directs me: the Act names no one who must give the notice, but the court cannot keep the matter until it is satisfied the notice was given and a reasonable time has passed (s 6(4))`

-- s 6(5)(a) (above). The ACT Act has no counterpart to the Commonwealth's s 6(7) (the Commonwealth paying a party's costs).
DECLARE `Can my adjournment costs be dealt with` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: the ACT Supreme Court may make such order as to the costs of the adjournment as it thinks fit (s 6(5)(a)); it is a discretion`
    `no: section 6(5)(a) deals only with the costs of an adjournment for the notice to the Attorneys-General`

@export My proceeding in the ACT Supreme Court was adjourned so the Attorneys-General could consider a special federal matter. Can my costs of the adjournment be dealt with?
GIVEN `my proceeding was adjourned for the section 6(4) notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the ACT Supreme Court adjourned your proceeding so that the Attorneys-General could consider the special federal matter (section 6(5)(a))
GIVETH A `Can my adjournment costs be dealt with`
DECIDE `Can my adjournment costs be dealt with question` IS
    IF `my proceeding was adjourned for the section 6(4) notice`
    THEN `possibly: the ACT Supreme Court may make such order as to the costs of the adjournment as it thinks fit (s 6(5)(a)); it is a discretion`
    ELSE `no: section 6(5)(a) deals only with the costs of an adjournment for the notice to the Attorneys-General`

-- s 6(7): "Nothing in this section prevents the Supreme Court granting urgent relief of an interlocutory nature if it is in
-- the interests of justice to do so."
DECLARE `Can I still get urgent interim relief` IS ONE OF
    `yes: section 6 does not stop the ACT Supreme Court granting me urgent interlocutory relief, as the court thinks the interests of justice require (s 6(7))`
    `section 6 does not stand in the way, but the ACT Supreme Court has not found that the interests of justice call for urgent interlocutory relief (s 6(7))`

@export My proceeding in the ACT Supreme Court involves a special federal matter that may have to go to the Federal Court. Can the Supreme Court still give me urgent interim relief?
GIVEN `the court thinks urgent interlocutory relief is in the interests of justice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the ACT Supreme Court considers that urgent interim relief (such as an injunction until the matter is decided) is in the interests of justice
GIVETH A `Can I still get urgent interim relief`
DECIDE `Can I still get urgent interim relief question` IS
    IF `the court thinks urgent interlocutory relief is in the interests of justice`
    THEN `yes: section 6 does not stop the ACT Supreme Court granting me urgent interlocutory relief, as the court thinks the interests of justice require (s 6(7))`
    ELSE `section 6 does not stand in the way, but the ACT Supreme Court has not found that the interests of justice call for urgent interlocutory relief (s 6(7))`

-- s 6(8): "If, through inadvertence, the Supreme Court determines a proceeding of the kind referred to in subsection (1)
-- without-- (a) the court making an order under subsection (3) ...; or (b) a notice mentioned in subsection (4) being given;
-- nothing in this section invalidates the decision of that court."
-- s 7(7): "If the Full Court of the Supreme Court-- (a) determines a proceeding to which subsection (4) applies as mentioned
-- in subsection (6); or (b) through inadvertence, determines a proceeding to which subsection (4) applies; nothing in this
-- section invalidates the decision of that court."
DECLARE `What went wrong` IS ONE OF
    `the ACT Supreme Court decided a special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or without the s 6(4) notice`
    `the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(4) sends to a federal Full Court or the High Court (through inadvertence, or because the interests of justice required it to proceed)`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Is the decision invalid` IS ONE OF
    `no: nothing in section 6 invalidates the decision (s 6(8))`
    `section 6(8) does not save it: it saves only a decision given through inadvertence; this Act does not say whether the decision is otherwise invalid`
    `no: nothing in section 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(7))`

@export The ACT Supreme Court decided my case when this Act says it should have gone elsewhere. Is the decision invalid for that reason?
GIVEN `what went wrong` IS A `What went wrong` @desc What happened
      `it happened through inadvertence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a special federal matter: whether the Supreme Court decided it without the order or notice through inadvertence (an oversight), rather than deliberately
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Is the decision invalid`
DECIDE `Is the decision invalid question` IS
    IF `what went wrong` EQUALS `the ACT Supreme Court decided a special federal matter without a s 6(3) order or without the s 6(4) notice`
    THEN IF `it happened through inadvertence`
         THEN `answered` `no: nothing in section 6 invalidates the decision (s 6(8))`
         ELSE `answered` `section 6(8) does not save it: it saves only a decision given through inadvertence; this Act does not say whether the decision is otherwise invalid`
    ELSE IF `what went wrong` EQUALS `the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court decided an appeal that s 7(4) sends to a federal Full Court or the High Court (through inadvertence, or because the interests of justice required it to proceed)`
         THEN `answered` `no: nothing in section 7 invalidates the decision (s 7(7))`
         ELSE `not covered` `this Act says nothing of that decision`

§§ `s 6A -- federal judicial review of a Commonwealth body acting under an ACT law`

-- s 6A(1): "This section applies to a proceeding (the federal matter proceeding) if-- (a) a matter for determination in the
-- proceeding is covered by [the Commonwealth definition of special federal matter], paragraph (c) or (e); and (b) the matter
-- ... involves or relates to the exercise, or purported or proposed exercise, of functions or powers given to a Commonwealth
-- authority, or officer of the Commonwealth, by an enactment (the ACT enactment) mentioned in the Administrative Decisions
-- (Judicial Review) Act 1977 (Cwlth), section 3 (1), definition of enactment, paragraph (ca) or (cb); and (c) the matter ...
-- arises out of, or relates to, another proceeding (the ACT matter proceeding) pending in any ACT court, if the ACT matter
-- proceeding-- (i) arises, or a substantial part of it arises, under the ACT enactment or a corresponding enactment of another
-- State; and (ii) does not involve a matter for determination covered by [paragraph (c) or (e)]; regardless of which
-- proceeding was begun first."
-- (8): "Commonwealth authority means an authority or other body (whether incorporated or not) that is established under a
-- Commonwealth Act." "officer of the Commonwealth--see the Commonwealth Constitution, section 75 (v)" (borrowed: asked).
-- (2), (3): in the Federal Court or Family Court: may transfer to the Supreme Court if appropriate; s 5(4) does not apply.
-- (4), (5): in the Supreme Court: ss 5(1) and 6 do not require transfer; the Supreme Court may transfer if appropriate.
DECLARE `What section 6A does for me` IS ONE OF
    `section 6A applies: the Federal Court or the Family Court may transfer my proceeding to the ACT Supreme Court if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (including having related proceedings heard together); s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2), (3))`
    `section 6A applies: sections 5(1) and 6 do not require the ACT Supreme Court to transfer my proceeding to the Federal Court or the Family Court, but it may do so if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(4), (5))`
    `no: section 6A does not apply to my proceeding (s 6A(1))`

@export I am seeking judicial review (ADJR Act or Judiciary Act s 39B) of a Commonwealth body or officer acting under an ACT law, and a related proceeding is pending in an ACT court. Does section 6A change where my case is heard?
GIVEN `my matter is under the ADJR Act or Judiciary Act s 39B` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in your proceeding is a special federal matter under paragraph (c) (the Administrative Decisions (Judicial Review) Act 1977) or (e) (section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903) of the Commonwealth definition
      `it concerns a Commonwealth body or officer acting under an ACT enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it involves the exercise of functions or powers given to a body established under a Commonwealth Act, or to an officer of the Commonwealth, by an ACT enactment of the kind in paragraph (ca) or (cb) of the ADJR Act's definition of "enactment"
      `a related proceeding under that ACT law is pending in an ACT court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your matter arises out of or relates to another proceeding pending in any ACT court that arises (wholly or substantially) under that ACT enactment or a corresponding enactment of another State
      `the ACT proceeding has no ADJR or s 39B matter in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that ACT proceeding involves no matter under the ADJR Act or section 39B of the Judiciary Act 1903
      `where my proceeding is` IS A `Court` @desc Where your judicial review proceeding is pending
GIVETH AN `Answer` `What section 6A does for me`
DECIDE `What section 6A does for me question` IS
    IF (NOT (`my matter is under the ADJR Act or Judiciary Act s 39B` AND `it concerns a Commonwealth body or officer acting under an ACT enactment` AND `a related proceeding under that ACT law is pending in an ACT court` AND `the ACT proceeding has no ADJR or s 39B matter in it`))
    THEN `answered` `no: section 6A does not apply to my proceeding (s 6A(1))`
    ELSE IF `is the Federal Court or the Family Court` `where my proceeding is`
         THEN `answered` `section 6A applies: the Federal Court or the Family Court may transfer my proceeding to the ACT Supreme Court if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (including having related proceedings heard together); s 5(4) does not apply (s 6A(2), (3))`
         ELSE IF `where my proceeding is` EQUALS `the ACT Supreme Court`
              THEN `answered` `section 6A applies: sections 5(1) and 6 do not require the ACT Supreme Court to transfer my proceeding to the Federal Court or the Family Court, but it may do so if it considers that appropriate in the interests of justice (s 6A(4), (5))`
              ELSE `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`

§§ `s 7 -- where must I bring my appeal (only if I want to appeal)`

-- s 7(1): "An appeal must not be instituted from a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court or the Family Court to the
-- Full Court of the Supreme Court." (2): "An appeal must not be instituted from the Federal Court or the Family Court to the
-- other of those courts."
-- (3): "If it appears that the only matters for determination in a proceeding by way of an appeal from a decision of a single
-- judge of the Supreme Court are matters other than matters arising under an Act specified in the Jurisdiction of Courts
-- (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Cwlth), schedule, that proceeding must be instituted only in, and must be determined only by, the
-- Full Court of the Supreme Court."
-- (4): "Subject to subsections (6) and (7), if it appears that a matter for determination in a proceeding by way of an
-- appeal from a decision of a single judge of the Supreme Court (not being a proceeding to which subsection (5) applies) is a
-- matter arising under an Act specified in the schedule ..., that proceeding must be instituted only in, and must be
-- determined only by-- (a) the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires; or (b) with
-- special leave of the High Court--the High Court."
-- (5): "A proceeding by way of an appeal from a decision of a judge of a State Family Court, being a proceeding involving the
-- determination of-- (a) a matter arising under an Act specified in the schedule ...; and (b) another matter; may be dealt
-- with as if no matter for determination in the proceeding were a matter arising under an Act specified in that schedule."
-- The Schedule is the Commonwealth Act's: a borrowed list, asked as a fact. "Full Court, in relation to a Supreme Court of a
-- State, includes a court of the State to which appeals lie from a single judge of that Supreme Court" (the ACT is a
-- "State" here, so the ACT Court of Appeal counts).
DECLARE `Decision I want to appeal` IS ONE OF
    `a decision of a single judge of the ACT Supreme Court`
    `a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court`
    `a decision of a single judge of the Family Court`
    `a decision of a judge of the Family Court of Western Australia`
    `a decision of another court`

DECLARE `Where must I appeal` IS ONE OF
    `to the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court only (s 7(3))`
    `to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court; not to the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court (s 7(4))`
    `not to the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court (s 7(1)), and not to the other of the Federal Court and the Family Court (s 7(2)); this Act does not say where it does lie`
    `the appeal may be dealt with as if no matter in it arose under a Schedule Act (s 7(5)); this Act does not otherwise say where it lies`

@export I want to appeal a judge's decision in a civil case connected with the ACT. Which court must I appeal to?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `Decision I want to appeal` @desc Whose decision you want to appeal
      `a matter in the appeal arises under an Act in the Schedule` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in the appeal arises under one of the Commonwealth Acts listed in the Schedule to the Jurisdiction of Courts (Cross-vesting) Act 1987 (Cwlth)
      `the appeal also involves another matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an appeal from the Family Court of Western Australia: whether the appeal also involves a matter that does not arise under a Schedule Act
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Where must I appeal`
DECIDE `Where must I appeal question` IS
    IF `the decision` EQUALS `a decision of a single judge of the ACT Supreme Court`
    THEN IF `a matter in the appeal arises under an Act in the Schedule`
         THEN `answered` `to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires, or with special leave to the High Court; not to the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court (s 7(4))`
         ELSE `answered` `to the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court only (s 7(3))`
    ELSE IF `the decision` EQUALS `a decision of a single judge of the Federal Court` OR `the decision` EQUALS `a decision of a single judge of the Family Court`
         THEN `answered` `not to the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court (s 7(1)), and not to the other of the Federal Court and the Family Court (s 7(2)); this Act does not say where it does lie`
         ELSE IF `the decision` EQUALS `a decision of a judge of the Family Court of Western Australia` AND `a matter in the appeal arises under an Act in the Schedule` AND `the appeal also involves another matter`
              THEN `answered` `the appeal may be dealt with as if no matter in it arose under a Schedule Act (s 7(5)); this Act does not otherwise say where it lies`
              ELSE `not covered` `this Act does not say where this appeal lies: that is for the Act that created the court`

-- s 7(6): "If-- (a) the Full Court of the Supreme Court commences to hear a proceeding by way of an appeal; and (b) before
-- the court determines the proceeding, it appears to it that the proceeding is a proceeding to which subsection (4) applies;
-- it must, unless the interests of justice require that it proceed to determine the proceeding, transfer the proceeding to
-- the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires."
DECLARE `What happens to my appeal` IS ONE OF
    `the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court must transfer my appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires (s 7(6))`
    `the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court may go on to decide my appeal, because the interests of justice require it; its decision is not invalid for that (s 7(6), (7)(a))`
    `section 7(6) does not apply: my appeal is not one that section 7(4) sends to a federal Full Court`

@export My appeal has started in the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court, but it involves a matter under an Act in the Commonwealth Act's Schedule. What happens to it?
GIVEN `it appears to the Full Court that a matter arises under a Schedule Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after the hearing began, it appeared to the Full Court that a matter in your appeal (from a single judge of the ACT Supreme Court) arises under an Act in the Schedule to the Commonwealth Act
      `the Full Court considers the interests of justice require it to proceed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Full Court considers that the interests of justice require it to go on and decide the appeal itself
GIVETH A `What happens to my appeal`
DECIDE `What happens to my appeal question` IS
    IF (NOT `it appears to the Full Court that a matter arises under a Schedule Act`)
    THEN `section 7(6) does not apply: my appeal is not one that section 7(4) sends to a federal Full Court`
    ELSE IF `the Full Court considers the interests of justice require it to proceed`
         THEN `the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court may go on to decide my appeal, because the interests of justice require it; its decision is not invalid for that (s 7(6), (7)(a))`
         ELSE `the Full Court of the ACT Supreme Court must transfer my appeal to the Full Court of the Federal Court or of the Family Court, as the case requires (s 7(6))`

§§ `s 8 -- removal into the ACT Supreme Court (only for a case in a lower ACT court or an ACT tribunal)`

-- s 8(1): "If-- (a) a proceeding (the relevant proceeding) is pending in-- (i) a court of the Australian Capital Territory,
-- other than the Supreme Court; or (ii) a tribunal established by or under an Act; and (b) it appears to the Supreme Court
-- that-- (i) the relevant proceeding arises out of, or is related to, another proceeding pending in the Federal Court, the
-- Family Court, the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory or a State Family Court and, if an order is made under
-- this subsection in relation to the relevant proceeding, there would be grounds on which the other proceeding could be
-- transferred to the Supreme Court; or (ii) an order should be made under this subsection in relation to the relevant
-- proceeding so that consideration can be given to whether the relevant proceeding should be transferred to another court;
-- the Supreme Court may, on the application of a party to the relevant proceeding or of its own initiative, make an order
-- removing the relevant proceeding to the Supreme Court." (2): "this Act applies in relation to the proceeding as if it were a
-- proceeding pending in the Supreme Court." (3): "the Supreme Court may, if it considers it appropriate to do so, remit the
-- proceeding to the court or tribunal from which the proceeding was removed."
DECLARE `Can my case be removed to the ACT Supreme Court` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: the ACT Supreme Court may order my proceeding removed to it, on my application or of its own initiative; this Act then applies as if it were pending in the Supreme Court, and the Supreme Court may later remit it (s 8(1)-(3))`
    `no: neither ground in section 8(1)(b) appears to the ACT Supreme Court`
    `no: section 8 does not reach a criminal proceeding (this Act's "proceeding" excludes it)`

@export My case is in a lower ACT court (such as the Magistrates Court) or an ACT tribunal, and a related case is in a federal court or another State's Supreme Court. Can my case be removed to the ACT Supreme Court?
GIVEN `the court or tribunal my case is in` IS A `Court` @desc Where your case is pending (choose "another ACT court, or a tribunal established under an ACT Act" for the Magistrates Court, ACAT and similar)
      `it is a criminal proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your case is a criminal prosecution
      `it appears to the Supreme Court my case is related to one elsewhere that could then come to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the ACT Supreme Court considers your case arises out of or is related to one pending in the Federal Court, the Family Court, another State's or a Territory's Supreme Court or a State Family Court, which could then be transferred to the ACT Supreme Court
      `it appears to the Supreme Court it should remove my case to consider a transfer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the ACT Supreme Court considers it should remove your case so that it can consider transferring it to another court
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can my case be removed to the ACT Supreme Court`
DECIDE `Can my case be removed to the ACT Supreme Court question` IS
    IF (NOT (`the court or tribunal my case is in` EQUALS `another ACT court, or a tribunal established under an ACT Act`))
    THEN `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
    ELSE IF `it is a criminal proceeding`
         THEN `answered` `no: section 8 does not reach a criminal proceeding (this Act's "proceeding" excludes it)`
         ELSE IF `it appears to the Supreme Court my case is related to one elsewhere that could then come to it` OR `it appears to the Supreme Court it should remove my case to consider a transfer`
              THEN `answered` `possibly: the ACT Supreme Court may order my proceeding removed to it, on my application or of its own initiative; this Act then applies as if it were pending in the Supreme Court, and the Supreme Court may later remit it (s 8(1)-(3))`
              ELSE `answered` `no: neither ground in section 8(1)(b) appears to the ACT Supreme Court`

§§ `s 10 -- sending an Australian Consumer Law claim to a lower ACT court`

-- s 10: "If-- (a) a proceeding is pending in the Federal Court, the Family Court, the Supreme Court or the Supreme Court of
-- another State or of a Territory; and (b) a matter for determination in the proceeding is a matter arising under the
-- Competition and Consumer Act 2010 (Cwlth), schedule 2, part 2-2, 3-1, 3-3 or 3-4, as the part applies as a law of the
-- Commonwealth; (c) no matter for determination in the proceeding is a special federal matter; and (d) the proceeding is not
-- a proceeding by way of an appeal from a judgment of a court; and (e) a court of the Australian Capital Territory, other
-- than the Supreme Court, has jurisdiction in respect of all matters for determination in the proceeding; the court referred
-- to in paragraph (a) may, on the application of a party to the proceeding or of its own initiative, transfer the proceeding
-- to the court referred to in paragraph (e)."
-- Australian Consumer Law parts: 2-2 (unconscionable conduct), 3-1 (unfair practices), 3-3 (safety of consumer goods and
-- product related services), 3-4 (information standards).
DECLARE `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court` IS ONE OF
    `possibly: the court may transfer my proceeding to the ACT court that has jurisdiction over all of it (such as the Magistrates Court), on my application or of its own initiative (s 10)`
    `no: no matter in my proceeding arises under the Australian Consumer Law parts section 10 names (s 10(b))`
    `no: a matter in my proceeding is a special federal matter (s 10(c))`
    `no: section 10 does not apply to an appeal (s 10(d))`
    `no: no ACT court other than the Supreme Court has jurisdiction over all the matters in my proceeding (s 10(e))`

@export My claim under the Australian Consumer Law (unconscionable conduct, unfair practices, consumer goods safety, information standards) is in a federal court or a Supreme Court. Can it be sent to a lower ACT court?
GIVEN `the court my proceeding is in` IS A `Court` @desc The court where your proceeding is pending
      `a matter arises under Australian Consumer Law part 2-2, 3-1, 3-3 or 3-4` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a matter in your proceeding arises under part 2-2, 3-1, 3-3 or 3-4 of the Australian Consumer Law (Competition and Consumer Act 2010, schedule 2) as a law of the Commonwealth
      `a matter in my proceeding is a special federal matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any matter in your proceeding is a special federal matter (see the section 6 question)
      `my proceeding is an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceeding is an appeal from a judgment of a court
      `a lower ACT court has jurisdiction over all of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an ACT court other than the Supreme Court (such as the Magistrates Court) has jurisdiction over every matter in your proceeding
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court`
DECIDE `Can my consumer claim go to a lower ACT court question` IS
    IF (NOT (`is the Federal Court or the Family Court` `the court my proceeding is in` OR `the court my proceeding is in` EQUALS `the ACT Supreme Court` OR `is the Supreme Court of another State or of a Territory, read with s 3` `the court my proceeding is in`))
    THEN `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
    ELSE IF (NOT `a matter arises under Australian Consumer Law part 2-2, 3-1, 3-3 or 3-4`)
         THEN `answered` `no: no matter in my proceeding arises under the Australian Consumer Law parts section 10 names (s 10(b))`
         ELSE IF `a matter in my proceeding is a special federal matter`
              THEN `answered` `no: a matter in my proceeding is a special federal matter (s 10(c))`
              ELSE IF `my proceeding is an appeal`
                   THEN `answered` `no: section 10 does not apply to an appeal (s 10(d))`
                   ELSE IF (NOT `a lower ACT court has jurisdiction over all of it`)
                        THEN `answered` `no: no ACT court other than the Supreme Court has jurisdiction over all the matters in my proceeding (s 10(e))`
                        ELSE `answered` `possibly: the court may transfer my proceeding to the ACT court that has jurisdiction over all of it (such as the Magistrates Court), on my application or of its own initiative (s 10)`

§§ `s 11 -- which law the court applies to my claim`

-- s 11(1): "If it appears to a court that the court will, or will be likely to, in determining a matter for determination in
-- a proceeding, be exercising jurisdiction invested or conferred by this Act or by a law of the Commonwealth or another State
-- relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction-- (a) subject to paragraphs (b) and (c), the court must, in determining that
-- matter, apply the law in force in the State or Territory where the court is sitting (including choice of law rules); and
-- (b) subject to paragraph (c), if that matter is a right of action arising under a written law of another State or
-- Territory, the court must, in determining that matter, apply the written and unwritten law of that other State or
-- Territory; and (c) the rules of evidence and procedure to be applied in dealing with that matter must be such as the court
-- considers appropriate in the circumstances, being rules that are applied in a superior court in Australia or in an external
-- Territory of Australia."
-- (2): "The reference in subsection (1) (a) to the State or Territory where the court is sitting is, in relation to the
-- Federal Court or the Family Court, a reference to the State or Territory where any matter for determination in the
-- proceeding was first commenced in or transferred to that court."
DECLARE `Which law applies to my claim` IS ONE OF
    `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law my right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
    `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
    `the law in force, including its choice of law rules, in the State or Territory where a matter in my proceeding was first commenced in or transferred to the Federal Court or the Family Court (s 11(1)(a), (2)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`

@export My claim is being heard by a court using cross-vested jurisdiction (for example after a transfer from the ACT to New South Wales). Which State's or Territory's law will the court apply?
GIVEN `it appears to the court it will exercise cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it appears to the court that, in deciding your matter, it will or is likely to be exercising jurisdiction given by this Act or by a Commonwealth or other State cross-vesting Act
      `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a right of action created by an Act or other written law of a State or Territory other than the one where the court sits (for example a statutory compensation claim under another State's Act)
      `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court hearing your matter
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Which law applies to my claim`
DECIDE `Which law applies to my claim question` IS
    IF (NOT `it appears to the court it will exercise cross-vested jurisdiction`)
    THEN `not covered` `the court is not exercising cross-vested jurisdiction, so section 11 does not say which law it applies`
    ELSE IF `my right of action arises under a written law of another State or Territory`
         THEN `answered` `the written and unwritten law of the other State or Territory under whose written law my right of action arises (s 11(1)(b)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
         ELSE IF `is the Federal Court or the Family Court` `the court`
              THEN `answered` `the law in force, including its choice of law rules, in the State or Territory where a matter in my proceeding was first commenced in or transferred to the Federal Court or the Family Court (s 11(1)(a), (2)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`
              ELSE `answered` `the law in force in the State or Territory where the court sits, including its choice of law rules (s 11(1)(a)); the rules of evidence and procedure are those the court thinks appropriate, from a superior court in Australia or an external Territory (s 11(1)(c))`

§§ `ss 11(3), 12, 13 -- after a transfer (only if my proceeding has been transferred or removed)`

-- s 11(3): "If a proceeding is transferred or removed to a court (the transferee court) from another court (the transferor
-- court), the transferee court must deal with the proceeding as if, subject to any order of the transferee court, the steps
-- that had been taken for the purposes of the proceeding in the transferor court (including the making of an order), or
-- similar steps, had been taken in the transferee court."
-- s 12: "If a proceeding is transferred or removed to a court, that court may make an order as to costs that relate to the
-- conduct of the proceeding before the transfer or removal if those costs have not already been dealt with by another
-- court."
DECLARE `What carries over after transfer` IS ONE OF
    `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); and the new court may order costs of the proceeding before the transfer or removal (s 12)`
    `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); the costs before the transfer or removal have already been dealt with by another court, so s 12 gives the new court no power over them`

@export My proceeding has been transferred or removed to another court. Do I have to start again, and who deals with the costs so far?
GIVEN `another court has already dealt with the costs before the transfer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another court has already made an order about the costs of the proceeding before it was transferred or removed
GIVETH A `What carries over after transfer`
DECIDE `What carries over after transfer question` IS
    IF `another court has already dealt with the costs before the transfer`
    THEN `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); the costs before the transfer or removal have already been dealt with by another court, so s 12 gives the new court no power over them`
    ELSE `the steps already taken (including orders made) count as taken in the new court, unless it orders otherwise (s 11(3)); and the new court may order costs of the proceeding before the transfer or removal (s 12)`

-- s 13: "An appeal does not lie from a decision of a court-- (a) in relation to the transfer or removal of a proceeding under
-- this Act; or (b) as to which rules of evidence and procedure are to be applied under section 11 (1) (c)."
DECLARE `Decision about my proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `a decision to transfer or remove my proceeding under this Act, or not to`
    `a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)(c)`
    `some other decision`

DECLARE `Can I appeal that decision` IS ONE OF
    `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
    `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)(c) (s 13(b))`

@export Can I appeal against the court's decision to transfer (or not transfer) my proceeding under this Act, or about the rules of evidence and procedure it will apply?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `Decision about my proceeding` @desc Which decision you want to appeal
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I appeal that decision`
DECIDE `Can I appeal that decision question` IS
    IF `the decision` EQUALS `a decision to transfer or remove my proceeding under this Act, or not to`
    THEN `answered` `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about the transfer or removal of a proceeding under this Act (s 13(a))`
    ELSE IF `the decision` EQUALS `a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)(c)`
         THEN `answered` `no: an appeal does not lie from a decision about which rules of evidence and procedure apply under s 11(1)(c) (s 13(b))`
         ELSE `not covered` `this Act says nothing of that decision`

§§ `s 14 -- enforcing my judgment in the ACT (only if I have a judgment)`

-- s 14(1): "A judgment of the Federal Court or the Family Court that is given, in whole or in part, in the exercise of
-- jurisdiction conferred by a law or laws relating to cross-vesting of jurisdiction is enforceable in the Australian Capital
-- Territory as if the judgment had been given entirely in the exercise of the jurisdiction of that court apart from any such
-- law." (2): the same for "A judgment of the Supreme Court". Dictionary: "judgment means a judgment, decree or order,
-- whether final or interlocutory."
DECLARE `Can I enforce my judgment in the ACT` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my Federal Court or Family Court judgment is enforceable in the ACT as if given entirely in that court's own jurisdiction (s 14(1))`
    `yes: my ACT Supreme Court judgment is enforceable in the ACT as if given entirely in the Supreme Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`

@export I have a judgment given (wholly or partly) under cross-vested jurisdiction. Can I enforce it in the ACT as an ordinary judgment of that court?
GIVEN `the court that gave my judgment` IS A `Court` @desc The court that gave the judgment (a judgment includes any decree or order, final or interim)
      `it was given wholly or partly under cross-vested jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judgment was given, in whole or in part, in jurisdiction conferred by this Act or another cross-vesting Act
GIVETH AN `Answer` `Can I enforce my judgment in the ACT`
DECIDE `Can I enforce my judgment in the ACT question` IS
    IF `it was given wholly or partly under cross-vested jurisdiction` AND `is the Federal Court or the Family Court` `the court that gave my judgment`
    THEN `answered` `yes: my Federal Court or Family Court judgment is enforceable in the ACT as if given entirely in that court's own jurisdiction (s 14(1))`
    ELSE IF `it was given wholly or partly under cross-vested jurisdiction` AND `the court that gave my judgment` EQUALS `the ACT Supreme Court`
         THEN `answered` `yes: my ACT Supreme Court judgment is enforceable in the ACT as if given entirely in the Supreme Court's own jurisdiction (s 14(2))`
         ELSE `not covered` `this provision of the Act does not speak to a proceeding in that court, or to that pair of courts`
